The problem asks us to count the number of pairs of mochi $(A_i, A_j)$ such that we can form a kagamimochi by placing $A_i$ on top of $A_j$. The condition for placing $A_i$ on top of $A_j$ is that the size of the top mochi ($A_i$) must be at most half the size of the bottom mochi ($A_j$). That is, $A_i \le \frac{A_j}{2}$, or equivalently $2 A_i \le A_j$.

We are given $N$ mochi with sizes $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_N$.
The mochi are arranged in ascending order of size, meaning $A_1 \le A_2 \le \dots \le A_N$.
We need to find the number of distinct pairs $(i, j)$ such that $A_i$ can be placed on top of $A_j$.
